content.text:
`InputArray` accepts both. The I/O types are:

```cpp
// You can pass double directly:
std::vector<cv::Point2d> polygon_double;
std::vector<cv::Point2d> intersection;
cv::intersectConvexConvex(polygon_double, polygon_double, intersection, false);
//                                                  ^ output is also Point2d
```

When the `Mat` backing is `CV_64FC2` (double), the internal processing stays `double` throughout. The float→double→float rounding only happens if you pass `Point2f` / `CV_32FC2`.

So in your code, just use `std::vector<cv::Point2d>` instead of `std::vector<cv::Point2f>`:

```cpp
std::vector<cv::Point2d> P_double, Q_double;
for (const auto& p : polygon.vertices()) {
    P_double.push_back(cv::Point2d(p.x(), p.y()));
}

// output
std::vector<cv::Point2d> result_double;
cv::intersectConvexConvex(P_double, Q_double, result_double, false);

// convert back
for (const auto& p : result_double) {
    result_verts.push_back(Point(p.x, p.y));
}
```

No precision loss at the input boundary, double arithmetic internally (in 4.12+), and double output. The API just happens to use `InputArray` (a union type) so both `Point2f` and `Point2d` work — the precision is entirely determined by what you pass in.

