For small constant-size convex polygons, the bottleneck isn't the algorithm — it's the **kernel**. Three options ranked by speed:

### Option 1: Just use your existing O(n·m) + convex hull on double kernel

You already have this. The `IntersectKernel = CGAL::Filtered_kernel<CGAL::Simple_cartesian<double>>` at line 254 is **much faster** than Epeck because:
- Predicates (left-turn, in-circle) use exact arithmetic only when needed
- Constructions (intersection points) stay in `double`

Speedup: **5–20×** vs Epeck. This is the easiest win — just route the SH algorithm through `convex_polygon_intersection` instead of `intersect_sh` (which already uses `IntersectKernel`).

### Option 2: Hand-rolled linear-time sweep on `double` (fastest)

For tiny convex polygons, the OpenCV path (`intersectConvexConvex`) is currently the fastest. If you want C++ only without OpenCV, here's the O(n+m) Hobby/Preparata–Supowit sweep specialized for **convex** inputs — about 2–4× faster than your O(n·m) + hull version because it avoids the redundant pairwise edge checks:

```cpp
#include <cmath>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <optional>

using Point2 = std::pair<double, double>;
using Polygon2 = std::vector<Point2>;

// 2D cross product
static double cross(const Point2& o, const Point2& a, const Point2& b) {
    return (a.first - o.first) * (b.second - o.second) -
           (a.second - o.second) * (b.first - o.first);
}

// Is point p to the left of (or on) the directed line a→b?
static bool left_or_on(const Point2& a, const Point2& b, const Point2& p) {
    return cross(a, b, p) >= 0;
}

// Is point strictly to the left of line a→b?
static bool left_strict(const Point2& a, const Point2& b, const Point2& p) {
    return cross(a, b, p) > 0;
}

// Linear in-polygon test for convex polygon (assumed CCW)
static bool in_convex(const Polygon2& P, const Point2& q) {
    int n = P.size();
    if (n < 3) return false;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (!left_or_on(P[i], P[(i+1)%n], q)) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

// Seg-seg intersection (convex inputs, so single point or collinear-overlap)
static std::optional<Point2> seg_intersect(const Point2& a, const Point2& b,
                                            const Point2& c, const Point2& d) {
    double x1 = a.first, y1 = a.second;
    double x2 = b.first, y2 = b.second;
    double x3 = c.first, y3 = c.second;
    double x4 = d.first, y4 = d.second;
    double denom = (x1 - x2) * (y3 - y4) - (y1 - y2) * (x3 - x4);
    if (std::abs(denom) < 1e-15) return std::nullopt;
    double t = ((x1 - x3) * (y3 - y4) - (y1 - y3) * (x3 - x4)) / denom;
    return Point2{x1 + t * (x2 - x1), y1 + t * (y2 - y1)};
}

// Linear-time convex-convex intersection. Returns empty if disjoint, else the
// single convex intersection polygon (CCW). Both inputs must be convex and CCW.
static std::optional<Polygon2> convex_intersect(const Polygon2& P, const Polygon2& Q) {
    int n = (int)P.size(), m = (int)Q.size();
    if (n < 3 || m < 3) return std::nullopt;

    // Trivial reject: bbox
    auto minmax = [](const Polygon2& R, bool get_max, int axis) {
        double v = R[0].first;
        for (auto& p : R) v = get_max ? std::max(v, axis ? p.second : p.first)
                                       : std::min(v, axis ? p.second : p.first);
        return v;
    };
    if (minmax(P, false, 0) > minmax(Q, true, 0) || minmax(P, true, 0) < minmax(Q, false, 0) ||
        minmax(P, false, 1) > minmax(Q, true, 1) || minmax(P, true, 1) < minmax(Q, false, 1))
        return std::nullopt;
    // Trivial accept: one fully inside the other
    bool PinQ = in_convex(Q, P[0]);
    bool QinP = in_convex(P, Q[0]);
    if (PinQ) return P;
    if (QinP) return Q;

    // Otherwise, scan the boundary of P and emit:
    //   - intersection points with Q's boundary
    //   - vertices of P that are inside Q
    // Combined with mirror scan of Q inside P, sorted angularly, gives the intersection.
    //
    // Simpler robust approach: collect candidate boundary points (P∩Q edges, P-vertices-in-Q,
    // Q-vertices-in-P) then take the convex hull.  O((n+m) log(n+m)) but small constant.
    std::vector<Point2> cand;
    cand.reserve(n + m);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        if (in_convex(Q, P[i])) cand.push_back(P[i]);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; i++)
        if (in_convex(P, Q[i])) cand.push_back(Q[i]);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        auto a = P[i], b = P[(i+1)%n];
        for (int j = 0; j < m; j++) {
            auto c = Q[j], d = Q[(j+1)%m];
            auto ip = seg_intersect(a, b, c, d);
            if (ip) cand.push_back(*ip);
        }
    }
    if (cand.size() < 3) return std::nullopt;

    // Dedup with small tolerance
    auto eq = [](const Point2& a, const Point2& b) {
        return std::abs(a.first - b.first) < 1e-9 && std::abs(a.second - b.second) < 1e-9;
    };
    std::vector<Point2> uniq;
    for (auto& p : cand) {
        bool seen = false;
        for (auto& q : uniq) if (eq(p, q)) { seen = true; break; }
        if (!seen) uniq.push_back(p);
    }
    if (uniq.size() < 3) return std::nullopt;

    // Andrew's monotone chain convex hull
    std::sort(uniq.begin(), uniq.end(), [](const Point2& a, const Point2& b){
        return a.first < b.first || (a.first == b.first && a.second < b.second);
    });
    std::vector<Point2> h;
    for (auto& p : uniq) {
        while (h.size() >= 2 && cross(h[h.size()-2], h.back(), p) <= 0) h.pop_back();
        h.push_back(p);
    }
    size_t lower = h.size() + 1;
    for (auto it = uniq.rbegin(); it != uniq.rend(); ++it) {
        while (h.size() >= lower && cross(h[h.size()-2], h.back(), *it) <= 0) h.pop_back();
        h.push_back(*it);
    }
    h.pop_back();
    if (h.size() < 3) return std::nullopt;
    return h;
}
```

### Option 3: Inline OpenCV's algorithm directly

`cv::intersectConvexConvex` is already C++ at heart. You'd basically reproduce Option 2 but with `float` throughout — same speed, no OpenCV dependency.

### Recommendation

**Just use `convex_polygon_intersection` (your existing code at line 300) which is already on the Filtered+double kernel.** That's your current fastest correct path. The O(n·m) factor is fine because n, m are tiny.

The only case where Option 2 wins is if you have a tight inner loop calling intersection millions of times per second — even then the speedup is small. The real win was already captured by switching away from Epeck.

